sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch09-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "If the underlying rules of the universe are causal invariant, then slices through the causal network at different angles, corresponding to different uniform velocities, are all governed by the same rules, so physics is the same regardless of uniform motion and the standard phenomena of special relativity such as time dilation follow.";
    dc:type "physical-hypothesis";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Causal invariance yields special relativity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 9, “Fundamental Physics”; section “Space, Time and Relativity” (from p. 516), p. 522. Scope: discrete causal network models of spacetime and special relativity"
}
